$DIR/main.p:10:3: cannot keep a[:] in first after this iteration: the loop at line 6 reuses a in every iteration
    a[:] is a slice that looks at a. a lives in main's frame, where every
    iteration of the loop at line 6 reuses the same memory for it. When the next
    iteration starts, first would see that iteration's a, not this one's.
    Go gives each iteration of a loop its own variables, so a function literal
    made in one iteration keeps that iteration's values. Plutonium keeps
    variables in the function's frame, which has room for one copy of each;
    giving every iteration its own copy would need memory for each iteration,
    allocated behind the program's back.
    If first is needed only in this iteration, use it before the iteration ends.
    To use the value later, keep the value instead of a view of it:
        var saved [2]int // declared before the loop
        ...
        saved = a
    (see "Loop iterations reuse their storage" in the Plutonium spec, section
    18.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3006" for a longer explanation)
